## What's next: build per child spec

Plans are written. The pipeline phase is `/aide:build` for each of the
7 aligned child specs, one fresh implementor agent per numbered step
(per the `feedback_build_one_agent_per_step.md` memory: never one
agent for the whole plan; never one agent across plans).

Build dependency chain (run plans in this order; do NOT parallelize
plans whose steps touch `src/types/index.ts` because three of them
collide on that file):

1. **`src/service/parseBrainAide/plan.aide`** (6 numbered steps) —
   foundation: collapses `BrainAideConfig` and `ParseBrainAideResult`,
   moves the read path to `.aide/config/brain.aide`, adds deprecated-
   field rejection, shrinks the `interpolateArgs` substitution map.
   Step 6 is the verify step and is EXPECTED to surface compile
   errors in 4 downstream files (`buildBrainState`, `writeMcpEntry`,
   `provisionBrain`, `obsidianBrainAideTemplate` tests). Record those
   failures and proceed; the next plan absorbs them.
2. **`src/service/buildBrainState/plan.aide`** (4 steps) —
   consumes the parser; redefines `BrainState` as the 4-state union;
   drops the `stat` step from the pipeline.
3. **`src/tools/brain/plan.aide`** (3 steps, 19 checkboxes) —
   consumes `BrainState`; deletes `INVALID_PATH_PROSE`; updates the
   `NO_BRAIN_AIDE_PROSE` path; retargets the defensive fallback.
4. **`src/tools/info/plan.aide`** (5 steps, 16 checkboxes) —
   forwards the new 4-state shape; adds `not.toHaveProperty`
   assertions for retired fields.
5. **`src/service/install/provisionBrain/plan.aide`** (4 steps) —
   rewrites the template (minimal frontmatter, `@bitbonsai/mcpvault`
   launcher); moves the scaffold path to `.aide/config/brain.aide`;
   codifies the no-overwrite invariant in JSDoc + Tier 3 comment.
6. **`src/cli/sync/plan.aide`** (5 steps) —
   updates path strings; rewrites the interpolation test for the
   default-no-op shape.
7. **`src/cli/init/plan.aide`** (9 steps) —
   path constants, brain-wiring prose collapse, ownership-boundary
   regression test. Last because it consumes most of the rest.

After each plan completes, run `/aide:qa` against the module's `.aide`
spec to verify code matches intent. Commit + push between plans (one
commit per child after its plan + build + qa cycle is green) — keeps
the dependency chain visible in git history and gives natural
checkpoints if a build phase needs to be re-attempted.

The plans collectively touch `src/types/index.ts` from three modules
(parseBrainAide, buildBrainState, tools/brain). Strictly serializing
the plans avoids races on that shared file.

---

## Anti-regression invariants the build phase must hold

These are the hard rules from `.aide/intent.aide` outcomes.undesired.
The build phase must not violate them, even if a quick path through a
ticket looks like it could:

- **No code path branches on `name`.** `name` is metadata. Different
  brains may share names without affecting code behavior. If a future
  consumer wants per-brain behavior, push it back into the user's
  prose — never into a registry.
- **No introspection of `mcpServerConfig.args`.** Don't extract a
  vault path, don't infer connector identity, don't stat any derived
  path. Reading args for any purpose other than passing them through
  to `.mcp.json` is the same regression as a `rootPath` field in
  different clothing.
- **No silent acceptance of deprecated fields.** `connector` /
  `rootPath` / `entryFile` / `tools` must be REJECTED with a
  `malformed-frontmatter` reason, not stripped on the floor.
- **No server-side substitution in the prose body.** Prose returns
  verbatim. Substitution lives only in `mcpServerConfig.args` at
  install/sync time.
- **No silent `.mcp.json` mutation.** The MCP server reads `.mcp.json`;
  it never rewrites it. Sync is the only mutation surface.
- **No overwrite of files under `.aide/config/` on subsequent
  `aide_init` / `aide_upgrade` runs.** Scaffolded once if absent;
  user-owned forever after. Same failure class as silent `.mcp.json`
  rewriting.
- **No new frontmatter field whose only consumer is the parser's
  own validation.** Every field must name a real runtime consumer
  (sync writes `mcpServerConfig`; the user and agent read `name` as
  a label). Anything else is validation theater.

---

## Working tree state

The repo currently shows uncommitted work from prior sessions plus the
in-progress alignment work. Build-phase commits should be ordered
along the dependency chain above (one commit per child after its plan
+ build + qa cycle). Ask the user before committing — cadence is a
judgment call worth confirming.

The package publishes via `node scripts/publish.mjs <patch|minor|major>`
which bumps versions, syncs `server.json`, tags, and lets CI handle
the npm + MCP Registry publish. Do NOT publish until the entire build
phase is green and the methodology doc rewrite has landed; an
intermediate publish would ship code and docs that disagree with each
other.

---

## Resume protocol for next session

1. Run `/aide` — orchestrator boots, runs `aide_info`, reads docs +
   intent tree.
2. Read this handoff to confirm the state above is still current.
3. Read `.aide/intent.aide` outcomes (the 11 brain-layer decisions
   above all live there) and the 7 aligned child `.aide` files for
   their slices of the contract.
4. Pick up at `/aide:build` against the next plan in the dependency
   ordering above. Start with `src/service/parseBrainAide/plan.aide`
   step 1 (the types collapse in `src/types/index.ts`). One fresh
   implementor agent per numbered step.
5. After each plan completes, run `/aide:qa` against that module's
   `.aide`. Commit + push between plans.

If the user asks "what's the state?" — answer: intent layer aligned
across root + 7 child specs, methodology doc rewritten, all 7 child
plans written, code not yet refactored, next phase is build per child
along the dependency chain (parseBrainAide first).
